<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bible</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bible is an anthology of religious texts central to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and important in other Abrahamic religions such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, compiled by different religious communities into official collections called biblical canons. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> with some parts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, is divided into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a>, Nevi'im and Ketuvim; its oldest parts may be as early as c. 1200 BCE. The Christian Bible joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-testament">Old Testament</a>, a reorganisation of the Hebrew Bible's books and the deuterocanonical books, to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> written mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek">Koine Greek</a> in the 1st century CE. With estimated sales of over five billion copies, it is the best-selling publication of all time.</p></div>
